{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcVAZbLBLJxKHSpwF48c", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Here's a regex that matches `YYYY-MM-DD` and checks that the month is 01\u201312 and the day is 01\u201331:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\n```\n\nHow it breaks down:\n- `\\d{4}`: a four-digit year.\n- `(0[1-9]|1[0-2])`: a month from 01 to 12.\n- `(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])`: a day from 01 to 31.\n- `^\u2026$`: the whole string must be the date. Remove these to find dates inside longer text, and use `\\b\u2026\\b` instead so it doesn't match part of a longer number.\n\nIt doesn't check whether the day exists in that month, so it accepts dates like `2026-02-30` or `2026-04-31`. If you need full validation, use the regex as a format check and then parse the result with a date function, for example `datetime.date.fromisoformat` in Python or `Temporal.PlainDate.from` in JavaScript.\n\nIf you only need the format and don't care about the ranges, `^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$` is enough."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7616, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16975, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7616}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cf29e3ab-891a-41bd-a3b4-ce1d8511f07c", "uuid": "f2a164e7-913d-4a7e-9a56-2856142891c7", "timestamp": "2026-10-02T00:45:38.613Z", "request_id": "req_011CfcVAZNwVBR4uLvXJoLN1"}
